hello  i'm going to briefly talk about breeding  for resistance to biotic damage in  vegetable crops specifically tomato  probiotic challenges could be bacterial  diseases fungal or myc diseases viral  diseases or insects which cause direct  damage by feeding or indirect damage by  transmission of pathogens such as virus  to control crop damage while maximizing  reduction in chemical sprays one must  control each of the biotic stresses  currently controlled by that spray  the question  one example  why are fungicides used in the northeast  field tomato production  the answer to control three different  diseases late blight early blight and  septoria leaf spot  therefore to protect the crop and its  quality while reducing fungicidal sprays  one must breed for resistance to each of  these three diseases  we were able to do that using previously  identified late blight and early blight  resistances  we identified and mapped additional qtl  for septoria resistance  created markers for all of these qtls to  be able to select for each of them in  segregating populations  ultimately creating lines containing all  of these resistances  this approach quickly created the  desired lines  in well-adapted backgrounds  now to support the release and  utilization of these lines multi-year  regional trials by wonderful  collaborators in new york pennsylvania  north carolina virginia  and west virginia tested experimental  hybrids created using these new fungal  resistant lines  and the trial results supported a very  rapid commercializations of hybrids  created using them  most importantly  the combination of the resistances  achieved up to a 70 reduction in the  number of sprays per season with the  greatest reduction seen in the seasons  that were favorable to the diseases  now the project was expanded repeating  the process by adding resistances to two  bacterial diseases bacterial spot and  bacterial spec  now interestingly we accidentally  discovered a new early blight resistant  qtl  in the process of handling the bacterial  spot while bacterial spot qtls  and this  early blight qtl was also transferred to  the new lines for even better control of  that disease  the resulting lines  combining fungal and bacterial  resistances are now released and  experimental hybrids are in regional  trials so this process of co-targeting  all the diseases controlled by the  fungicide succeeded  we'll switch to a contrasting example  control of insects and insect  transmitted virus  now again we've got a question why are  pesticides heavily used on tomato crops  in warm climates the answer is control  of insects and insect transmitted virus  which are devastating  exacerbating the situation is that  insects and viruses can both have wide  host ranges so it's hard if not  impossible to eliminate them in your  environment  the pesticides are expensive lose their  efficacy  and have negative impacts on the  environment so a different method was  needed  we targeted acyl sugars which are insect  resistant compounds found in trichome  exudates in some wild solenaceous  species  the selenium pineally eye produces large  amounts you can see these droplets on  the trichomes  the exudate is about 95 ace of sugar  acyl sugars are effective against many  different species of insects so in this  case the resistance system itself has  considerable breadth of action  we're not having to transfer resistance  to each insect separately  where you're using one broad mechanism  to target several insects  the acyl sugars act on contact  with the insects  causing feeding and over position  deterrence  since insects must feed to transmit  virus  strongly reducing feeding could reduce  virus inoculation  the first tomatoes producing high levels  of acid sugar were  cu97fl and then from a series of sister  lines we chose cuo71026  now what is immediately obvious here is  that under heavy field pressure this was  in florida  there are over 100 110  um  flies per leaflet of unprotected tomato  however they're less than two  white fly per leaflet on most of the  acyl sugar lines  now all of these acyl sugar lines  produce the same form of basil sugar  which is quite different from its  pinellas parent  and among the different accessions of  panelia you'll find accessions that  produce different forms of basil sugar  so the question  comes up  which is important is a sugar level iso  sugar type or a combination  to examine this question  we isolated and purified acyl sugars  from four accessions of pinelli two from  the northern extent into the southern  extent of its range  and also from o71026  and then applying equal molar levels of  the  acyl sugars to an appropriate feeding  platform for each of the insects tested  we would ask the question  what is the impact and the differences  in the impacts of the different acel  sugars  well 071026 is an acyl sucrose with  branched chain fatty acids based on an  iso c5 platform 5 carbon  716 which was parental to  071026 and 1732 are southern accessions  of pinelli and they produce acyl  glucoses that are based on iso c4  um platform quite different  the northern range um accessions of  pinelli  these two  produce a 50 50 mix of acyl sucrose and  nasal glucose and have fatty acids that  are more similar to those in o7 1026  and you can see the response of in this  case western flower thrip to the acyl  sugars was quite different  if you just look across the side  horizontally here  about 2.5  milligrams per milliliter solution was  used  in this case could produce that degree  of control where you'd have to go up to  about five to get the same level control  with the southern accessions of pinelli  and all the way over to 16 or 17  milligrams per milliliter in order to  get similar control from 0.71026  so acyl sugar type definitely affects  the efficacy of the compound  this gives us the prediction that tomato  breeders can optimize as mediated  control of insects by increasing acell  sugar level  or control or both  so we needed a way to have tomato lines  producing acid sugars that were  different in level or type  first we identified the qtl which would  affect basil sugar level or type  then back cross them into  071026 to create this series of sister  lines  for example a qtl on chromosome 6 al6  almost doubles acid sugar level but the  acyl sugar is the same as that of o7126  and opposite qtl is fa5 which almost  blocks associated production producing a  line that is a wonderful control for the  other sister lines  fatty acid 2 fatty acid 7 or qtl that  either increase in the case of fatty  acid 2 or decrease the length of some of  the fatty acid side chains of the acyl  sugar and when combined increase acyl  sugar level 90 to 95 percent  and an addition of three or four qtl was  necessary to create an acyl glucose line  these lines were used by collaborating  entomologists and virologists to  determine the levels and types of acyl  sugars that best control different  important pests and their associated  transmitted virus  and i will just go in two examples  first is western flower threat which  transmits tomato spotted wild virus  if we look at over position  to determine the degree of deterrence  on the different lines you can see that  there is a great deal of opposition on  standard tomato and even on the line  that has blocked acyl sugar production  0.71026 and the other sister lines have  much lower levels of oval position with  some of the sister lines being  significantly lower than 0.71026  looking at  the um probability of inoculation from 0  to 100  you see it's very high in tomato and  071026 itself is not as good as would be  desired for controlling inoculation by  tomato spotted wilt virus but some of  the sister lines are quite good  similarly looking at silver leaf white  fly famicia which transmits tomato  yellow leaf girl virus  the first trials were field cage trials  with a few lines that raised acid sugar  level as you can see here  the qtl6 is also associated with an  increase in trichome density which no  doubt has some of the some of the reason  for the increased acyl sugar level  as you can see we had very high levels  of white fly  on the tomato control  but  it was strongly decreased in 0.71026  and significantly further reduced  in the sister lines with higher levels  of basal sugar so higher levels of basal  sugar  increase suppression deterrence of the  insects  in another series of tests looking at  tomato yellow leaf curl virus  transmission and inoculation into plants  by the solar leaf white plant  now this part of the slide is showing  the percent infection which gets very  high for tomato  but is lower for most of the different  tomato asosure lines tested  and significantly lower for some of them  in this case the researchers went one  step further and looked at acquisition  of tylcb by a  whitefly that initially was clean was  not carrying virus  and so we had some virus infected plans  of each type of each line  and what they were doing  was looking at whether or not the white  flies became voliferous acquired the 2i  lcd  and there were some acquiring it for any  line but they were lower for the acyl  sugar lines and significantly lower for  some  in that  series of experiments  there are more experiments going on with  these and other insects and insect  transmitted virus  now in order for this trait to be used  commercially it has to be used by the  seed companies in their breeding  ce071026n9 97fl could not be used by  seed companies because there's just too  much pinelli  dna in their genome and some of the  integrations are so large that they are  carrying  genes for negative plant or fruit traits  so in the past several years we've been  selecting for shorter forms of the  integrations and testing to  ascertain that they have retained their  acyl sugar qtl but have lost their  association with negative qtl that are  affecting plantar fruit type  we are now combining these short  integrations pyramiding them as it were  into final lines that should produce the  level and type of acid sugars needed to  control insect and  insect transmitted virus  these final lines will have genomes that  are only two to three percent pinelli  compared to 25 percent in cu 97fl and 12  in  c071026  substantially less wild species dna  which is why the plants are looking much  more  normal  the type of lines we are producing are  shown below there's a class a and a  class b that differ slightly with the  qtls on chromosome 3 but are otherwise  similar  thank you very much  and i will just leave it with this slide  showing wonderful collaboration by  entomologists and biologists at  different institutions  you

